<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Monaco</h1><p class="st-meta">1297 CE – present · capital Monaco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A rock on the Riviera seized by the Grimaldis in 1297 and still ruled by them, now one of the world's smallest and richest sovereign states.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In January 1297 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-grimaldi">François Grimaldi</a>, a Genoese Guelph exile, took the fortress of Monaco disguised as a Franciscan friar, an event recalled in the monk on the family arms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-grimaldi">Grimaldis</a> lost and regained the rock several times before establishing lasting control in the 15th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-grimaldi">Grimaldis</a> governed as lords, later princes, balancing between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Savoy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honore-ii-prince-of-monaco">Honoré II</a> placed Monaco under French protection in 1641 and adopted the title of prince. Revenues came from tolls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and citrus from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-cities-of-menton-and-roquebrune">Menton and Roquebrune</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-prince-of-monaco">Charles III</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-carlo">Monte Carlo</a> casino in 1863.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Monaco was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1793 and restored in 1814 under Sardinian protection. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-cities-of-menton-and-roquebrune">Menton and Roquebrune</a> broke away in 1848 and were sold to France in 1861. Constitutions of 1911 and 1962 limited princely power; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainier-iii-prince-of-monaco">Rainier III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-prince-of-monaco">Albert II</a> it became a banking and tourism centre.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-monaco.jpg" alt="Principality of Monaco" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by User:SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Monaco.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1297 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Monaco</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>François Grimaldi, Honoré II of Monaco, Rainier III of Monaco</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Monaco</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Monaco, Lordship of Monaco</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1662&amp;focus=principality-of-monaco" aria-label="Principality of Monaco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4858 628 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Monaco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5178 884L4910 989M5178 884L5054 728M5178 884L5054 728M5178 884L5226 1028M5178 884L5180 883"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M5054 728h0M5054 728h0M5226 1028h0M5180 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5178 884h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5178 884h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Monaco. <a href="/atlas?year=1662&amp;focus=principality-of-monaco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
